  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00120:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Oxidation and Reduction in Organic Chemistry"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "6a14cec5-46ed-4f74-9bfe-a32cbe924220"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00120:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Via oxidation, methane reacts with chlorine to form chloromethane and HCl. Via reduction, chloromethane reacts with Mg, ether, and hydronium ions to form methane."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_08_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00120:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "Via oxidation, ethylene reacts with bromine to form 1,2-Dibromoethane. Via neither oxidation nor reduction, ethylene reacts with HBr to form bromoethane."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_08_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00120:caption:fig-00003-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00120:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Fourteen compounds are listed in order of increasing level of oxidation. Alkanes have lowest whereas CO2 and CCl4 have the highest level. Other compounds fall between these."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_10_08_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00120:para:para-00006"
    },
